
  Each is its own spec → plan → build. I'd brainstorm one of them properly now and leave the others as one-line
  sketches.

  [1mThe fork is which one we spec first[0m, and it's a real choice because it changes what you can see working and when:

  - [1mBottom-up (1 → 5 in order).[0m Spec the task model and UI now; you get a working single-user task app quickly, then
    layer server, accounts, and notifications onto it. Buys the fastest visible progress and the least wasted work if
    priorities shift. Costs: you don't see a notification for a long time, and layering multi-user on afterward means
    revisiting the data model once (manageable if we design it multi-user-aware from the start).
  - [1mWalking skeleton (thin slice through all five).[0m Build the narrowest end-to-end path that actually delivers one
    notification: one task, two hardcoded users, one change type, one in-app toast. Buys early proof that the hard part
    — the change → subscription → delivery pipeline — works, and de-risks the architecture. Costs: more upfront
    infrastructure before anything is usable, and the task UI stays crude for a while.
  - [1mNotifications-first design, build last.[0m Spec the whole notification subsystem now while it's fresh, then build 1–3
    against that known target. Buys a data model shaped correctly for events from day one. Costs: it's a spec for code
    you can't write for weeks, and specs age — this is the one I'd avoid.

  Recommendation: [1mwalking skeleton.[0m Your actual risk isn't "can we render a task list" — it's the event pipeline, and
  the sequencing question you can't answer yet is what delivery should feel like. A thin slice puts a real notification
  in front of you in the shortest time, and it's the cheapest place to discover that, say, email is overkill and an
  in-app badge is all you need. Bottom-up is the safer pick if you mainly want a usable task app soon and notifications
  can wait.
